35,2,1,talk,Toshio,,"The data reveals a clear disconnect. Specialist rooms remain largely idle, while general-purpose rooms are overcapacity. Furthermore, projects that benefited a single wing were rated poorly compared to those with open access.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,1,talk,Victoria,,"My findings align with that. I discovered that every room taken out of service for works required expensive off-site hired space for two terms, a cost that is often overlooked during the planning phase.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,1,talk,George,,It is concerning that our bench technicians are still spending hours on manual checks and window-opening. This is a waste of their expertise and a sign that our equipment is outdated.,"{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,1,talk,Mizuki,,"My findings suggest a pattern: projects driven by comfort or appeal frequently slip by a year or more, whereas those addressing inspection findings stay on track. We must be wary of overly ambitious plans.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,1,talk,Benjamin,,"It appears we are all discovering that our ""paper"" reality is quite different from the actual one. I found that mechanical plant costs have surged by twenty percent, far outpacing our standard indices.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,2,talk,Toshio,,"It seems we are facing a systemic inefficiency. We have specialized spaces that are underutilized and general spaces that are insufficient, while the projects that failed to provide broad access were the least valued.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,2,talk,Victoria,,"Exactly. If we ignore the financial and logistical burden of replacing rooms during construction, we are underestimating the true cost of these improvements. We must prioritize plans that minimize this disruption.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,2,talk,George,,We must prioritize upgrades that automate these manual burdens. It is unacceptable that skilled technicians are wasting their time on basic window-opening routines simply because the equipment is obsolete. We need a practical solution.,"{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,2,talk,Mizuki,,"While automation is a goal, George, we must be realistic. If we choose a complex mechanical upgrade, Benjamin's data on rising costs and my findings on schedule slips suggest it could easily spiral out of control.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""George"",""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,2,talk,Benjamin,,"Mizuki's caution is well-founded. If we pursue a complex mechanical overhaul, we are essentially inviting a budget overrun that our current indices are blissfully unaware of. We are dancing on a very thin financial tightrope.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Mizuki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,3,talk,Toshio,,"If we prioritize a plan that converts underutilized specialist space into general-purpose areas, we could address the capacity crisis and improve user satisfaction without necessarily triggering the cost overruns Benjamin fears.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,3,talk,Victoria,,"That sounds promising, Toshio. However, we must ensure such a conversion doesn't trigger the same off-site hiring costs I found. A plan is only efficient if the transition doesn't cripple the current timetable.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,3,talk,George,,"Conversion is all well and good, but it doesn't solve the burden on our technicians. We need a plan that specifically addresses the outdated equipment causing these manual routines. Efficiency isn't just about room usage.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,3,talk,Mizuki,,"I understand your point, George, but we must be careful. If the equipment upgrade involves the mechanical plant costs Benjamin mentioned, it risks becoming another project that slips and exceeds its budget. We need a balanced approach.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""George"",""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,3,talk,Benjamin,,"A balanced approach is a lovely sentiment, Mizuki, but balance often looks like compromise on paper and chaos in practice. We must avoid any plan that relies on the fantasy of fixed mechanical costs.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Mizuki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,4,talk,Toshio,,"We must look for a plan that maximizes accessibility and utility while minimizing mechanical complexity. A project that focuses on flexible, general-purpose space seems the most rational way to ensure broad satisfaction and financial stability.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,4,talk,Victoria,,"I agree. We need a plan that balances broad utility with minimal disruption. If we can find a proposal that avoids extensive mechanical work and minimizes room downtime, we protect both the budget and the timetable.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,4,talk,George,,"I cannot agree to ignore the staff. If we only focus on room flexibility and budget, we continue to neglect the people keeping this place running. We must choose a plan that reduces manual labor.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,4,talk,Mizuki,,"George, I sympathize with the staff, but we cannot ignore the data. A plan that fails to deliver or bankrupts us helps no one. We must prioritize a proposal that is realistically executable and financially stable.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,4,talk,Benjamin,,It seems we have reached a classic impasse between operational necessity and financial reality. Let us hope the candidates provide a solution that doesn't require us to ignore either the technicians or the bank balance.,"{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
