60,3,1,talk,Toshio,,"Based on the data, Option B is the most rational choice. It converts underused space into general-purpose areas, maximizing accessibility and user satisfaction while avoiding the mechanical risks and costs Benjamin and Mizuki highlighted.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Benjamin"",""Mizuki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,1,talk,Victoria,,"Option B is indeed strong, but Option D also offers broad accessibility through its booking system. However, B minimizes the risk of room downtime and off-site hiring costs, making it the most sustainable for the timetable.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,George,,Option A is the only choice that addresses the operational burden. Replacing the ventilation and extraction equipment will eliminate those wasteful manual routines and finally provide our technicians with the tools they need.,"{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,Mizuki,,"Option A is far too risky. Between the mechanical cost surges Benjamin found and my data on schedule slips for ""appeal"" projects, it's a recipe for disaster. Option B is the most realistic and executable choice.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Benjamin,,"Option A is a financial siren song; those mechanical costs will devour our budget before the first vent is installed. Option B, however, is delightfully simple and avoids the market's current volatility. I vote for B.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,Toshio,,"Option D is a tempting alternative for accessibility, but it creates a new specialist hub. Option B addresses the actual shortage of general-purpose space, which the booking data proved is our most critical deficiency.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Victoria,,"I agree. Option D might be useful, but Option B directly solves the capacity crisis without the risk of displacing existing classes or incurring the heavy off-site costs associated with more complex renovations.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,George,,You are all prioritizing convenience and budgets over the people who actually maintain this campus. Option A is not a luxury; it is a necessity to stop the burnout of our technicians. We cannot keep ignoring them.,"{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Mizuki,,"George, I truly care about the staff's well-being, but pushing for a project that is likely to fail or overrun doesn't help them. Option B is a safe, achievable win that improves the campus for everyone.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Benjamin,,"George, your passion is admirable, but passion is a poor hedge against inflation. Option A is a fiscal black hole. Option B is the only choice that doesn't treat our budget as a mere suggestion.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,Toshio,,"The evidence is clear. Option B aligns with the user ratings for open access and solves the general-purpose room shortage. It is the only proposal that satisfies our operational, financial, and user-experience constraints simultaneously.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,Victoria,,"I concur. Option B avoids the high-risk mechanical costs and, crucially, prevents the timetable disruptions and off-site expenses that plague more invasive projects. It is the most responsible choice for the university's current state.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,George,,I cannot in good conscience support a plan that simply rearranges furniture while our technicians are left to struggle with obsolete equipment. We must address the actual labor burden. I stand by Option A.,"{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,Mizuki,,"We must be honest about our capacity. Option A is a high-risk gamble with our budget and timeline. Option B is a practical, achievable improvement that benefits the entire community without risking a total collapse of the schedule.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Benjamin,,"It is a rare pleasure to see such a consensus emerge. While George's concerns are poignant, we cannot build a sanctuary for staff on a foundation of financial fantasy. Option B is the only logical path. I vote for B.","{""words"":40,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,4,talk,Toshio,,"I have considered all perspectives, but the data on underutilized specialist space and the demand for general rooms is undeniable. Option B is the only choice that ensures broad utility and financial stability. I vote for B.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,4,talk,Victoria,,I have weighed the risks of disruption and the need for accessibility. Option B provides the most utility with the least impact on the timetable and budget. I vote for B.,"{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
77,3,4,talk,George,,"I have listened to you all, but I cannot ignore the human cost. We cannot continue to prioritize convenience over the basic operational needs of our staff. For the sake of our technicians, I vote for Option A.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
78,3,4,talk,Mizuki,,"I have carefully weighed the risks. Option A is a gamble we cannot afford, while Option B is a realistic, sustainable improvement. For the stability of our budget and schedule, I vote for B.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
79,3,4,talk,Benjamin,,"It appears the verdict is reached. While I sympathize with George's plight, logic dictates we avoid a fiscal catastrophe. For the sake of our sanity and our solvency, I cast my final vote for Option B.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
